## Conclusion

GPT-6.1 Sol is OpenAI's mid-priced GPT-6 model, released September 29, 2026, designed for complex coding, computer use, and professional work with near-Astra intelligence at one-fifth of the cost. It features a massive 1.05M token context window, competitive pricing ($2/$10 per 1M tokens), and exceptional performance across agentic coding and computer use benchmarks. The model ranks #7 of 27 models on the Vals Index with 63.94% and achieves 52 on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index (comparable to GPT-6 Astra). GPT-6.1 Sol delivers substantial gains over its predecessor GPT-6 Sol, more than doubling Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1 score at maximum reasoning effort while costing less than half per task.

However, detailed public information about GPT-6.1 Sol is limited compared to other frontier models from Western companies. The model's strengths in cost efficiency, agentic coding, and scientific performance make it a strong contender in the current market, particularly for organizations needing cost-effective frontier-level performance for complex, multi-hour workflows. The availability of GPT-6 Astra (more capable but 80% more expensive) and GPT-6.1 Terra (even cheaper but potentially less capable) suggests that OpenAI is offering a tiered approach to frontier models with different trade-offs between capability and accessibility.

For organizations heavily invested in the OpenAI ecosystem and seeking cost-effective frontier-level performance for complex, multi-hour workflows, GPT-6.1 Sol could be a viable option. For organizations requiring maximum accuracy for critical tasks, GPT-6 Astra may be more suitable. For organizations outside OpenAI ecosystem, established models from OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google may offer better value and transparency.

**Overall Assessment:** Excellent cost efficiency and strong agentic coding capabilities for complex, multi-hour workflows; however, limited public documentation and benchmark data compared to other frontier models, and less capable than GPT-6 Astra for maximum accuracy tasks.
